# chibi-math-stats: use the file's own seq-every, not SRFI 1's every
#
# `spearman-rank-correlation` calls `map-rank`, which returns a sequence of the
# same kind it was given -- a vector here -- and then applies SRFI 1's `every`
# to it. `every` is list-only, so this is a defect in `stats.scm` rather than a
# strictness difference between implementations. Patina raises "null-list?:
# argument out of domain", which is not a message we invented: it comes from
# the SRFI's own reference implementation, where `null-list?` is *specified* to
# raise on anything neither pair nor null. Gauche raises too. See #376.
#
# **This is a slip, not a design choice, which is what makes it patchable.**
# `stats.scm` defines a whole `seq-*` family under the comment "Sequence
# utilities - all procedures handle both lists and vectors", including
# `seq-every` -- and uses `(seq-every exact-integer? ...)` for this exact check
# in three other places (lines 284, 289, 353). Line 812 is the one site that
# reaches for the bare SRFI 1 name. The rewrite is the file's own generic,
# spelled the way its author spells it everywhere else.
#
# **It also fixes a live wrong answer, not just a raise.** chibi accepts the
# vector and returns `#t` -- measured, it returns `#t` even for
# `(every exact-integer? (vector 1 'x 3))`, never inspecting the elements. That
# `every` pair is the guard choosing between the no-ties shortcut and
# `pearson-correlation`, so on chibi the tie branch is dead and a *tied*
# distribution silently gets the wrong coefficient. `seq-every` answers
# correctly: `#t` with no ties, `#f` when averaged ranks make them inexact.
#
# Measured after: 117 of 117 assertions pass, up from 116. The suite's own data
# has no ties, so the assertion that was failing is not the one this protects --
# which is why the bug has gone unnoticed upstream.
diff -ruN a/chibi/math/stats.scm b/chibi/math/stats.scm
--- a/chibi/math/stats.scm
+++ b/chibi/math/stats.scm
@@ -809,8 +809,8 @@
           (not (distribution-pure? dist2)))
   (let ((r1 (map-rank dist1 <))
         (r2 (map-rank dist2 <)))
-    (if (and (every exact-integer? r1)
-             (every exact-integer? r2))
+    (if (and (seq-every exact-integer? r1)
+             (seq-every exact-integer? r2))
         ;; shortcut when there are no ties
         (let ((len (size dist1)))
           (- 1 (/ (* 6 (square-sum (seq-map - r1 r2)))
